This is a major refactor of splitflapfirmwarev7, splitting out functionality
into separate files and cleaning the RS-485 parsing logic.

This was written and tested on an ATTiny816 instead of an ATTiny1616 like
the original. If you want to use this on an larger ATTiny board, simply change
the FQBN tags in the sketch.yaml file to match your board. If your board has a
different pinout than mine, you may also have to change which pins are used
in the various files.

!! BEWARE !!
If you decide to change the pinouts, especially for the debug serial output
and the RS-485 RX/TX, make sure the pins you're using support this
functionality.

For my board, this is how I ended up organizing my pins. The Arduino breakout
board I was using for testing and development doesn't break everything out,
so I didn't have everything at my disposal.

Function    | Arduino Pin | ATTiny816 Pin
------------|-------------|--------------
RS-485 TX   |          14 | PA1
RS-485 RX   |          15 | PA2
RS-485 DE   |           0 | PA4
UPDI        |   Unlabeled | PA0
Home sensor |             | PC0
Debug TX    |          11 | PC1
Status LED  |             | PC2
Motor IN1   |           7 | PB2
Motor IN2   |           6 | PB3
Motor IN3   |           5 | PB4
Motor IN4   |           4 | PB5

Testing
-------
tests/run_tests.sh builds the firmware on your computer, against fake
hardware, and runs its tests. It needs CMake 3.24 or later and a C++17
compiler (g++ or clang++). The tests use GoogleTest, which the first run
downloads unless it's installed. Building takes a few seconds; the tests
take well under one.

The firmware's own source files are compiled as they are. What's faked is
in tests/fakes: the clock, the RS-485 serial port, the EEPROM, and a reel
that the real motor code turns and the real home sensor code reads. A test
puts messages on the bus, lets simulated time pass, and checks where the
reel ended up and what the module sent back. Pass part of a test's name to
run just those tests, e.g. tests/run_tests.sh Frame.

Each test runs in its own process (CTest runs them one by one), so each
starts from a freshly powered-on module with blank EEPROM. Running the
test program directly runs them all in one process instead, where they'd
see each other's state; use run_tests.sh or ctest.

check_flash.sh builds the firmware for the ATtiny816 with arduino-cli, with
debug output and without (DEBUG_OUTPUT 0, as flashed), and reports how much
of the 8 KB of flash and 512 bytes of RAM each uses. It fails if either
doesn't fit, and warns when one has less than 256 bytes of flash left.

Both run on GitHub for every pull request that changes the firmware
(.github/workflows/firmware.yml), with the sizes in the run's summary.
